  "WebhooksEvidence": "ens-notification-signing.json — explicit statement: \"Marketing Cloud Event Notification Service signs every notification delivered to your callback with an HMAC-SHA256 signature... compare the HMAC-SHA256 signature to the value in x-sfmc-ens-signature.\" ENS callback/subscription CONFIGURATION is in-scope as CRUD records (createCallback/createSubscription/etc.); the inbound webhook DELIVERY transport itself is out of scope (see OutOfScopeObjectFamilies).",
  "BulkOperationsAvailable": true,
  "BulkOperationsBasePath": "/data/v1/async",
  "BulkOperationsEvidence": "sources/rest-parsed.json — insertDataExtensionIDAsync (POST /data/v1/async/dataextensions/{id}/rows) and updateDataExtensionIDAsync (PUT, same path) submit a job; getStatusDataExtensionAsync (GET /data/v1/async/{requestId}/status) and getResultsDataExtensionAsync (GET /data/v1/async/{requestId}/results) poll it — a genuine async submit-then-poll pattern, distinct from the SOAP-side Perform/Schedule/Configure action verbs (see ActionCatalogSummary).",
  "TokenRefreshStrategy": "proactive-before-expiry",
  "TokenRefreshStrategyNote": "See Auth.refreshPolicy/Auth.tokenLifetimeSeconds for the full evidence — restated here at the top level because TokenRefreshStrategy is named as its own Configuration slot in the extraction directive.",
  "WriteCapability": {
    "soap": "Create/Update/Delete verbs exist per the CRUD matrix (sources/soap-crud-matrix.json, 85 unique object names with Create/Retrieve/Update/Delete/Perform/Schedule/Configure columns) — write support is genuinely PER-OBJECT, not uniform. Tracking events (SentEvent, OpenEvent, etc.) are Retrieve-only by their nature (they are system-generated log records); DataExtensionObject/DataExtension/Contact-adjacent objects (Subscriber, List) support Create/Update/Delete per the matrix.",
    "rest": "Create/Update(PATCH or PUT)/Delete verbs observed per-resource in sources/rest-parsed.json (Methods: GET 75 / POST 76 / PATCH 5 / PUT 13 / DELETE 20) — again per-object, not uniform. Some REST families are read-only or action-only (see OutOfScopeObjectFamilies action-not-record-crud entry and ActionCatalogSummary).",
    "note": "Per-IO WriteCapability (hasCreate/hasUpdate/hasDelete) is captured per-object in sources/capabilities.json by ioiof-extractor''s own evidence; this vendor-level note establishes that write capability gates on the OBJECT, never assume vendor-wide uniform CRUD."
  },
  "ConcurrencyControl": "none",
  "ConcurrencyControlEvidence": "A corpus-wide scan of all 865 pinned Atlas pages (excluding manifest.json) for the literal strings ''ETag'' and ''If-Match'' returned 0 hits on both (see CODE_EVIDENCE — extract-sfmc-facts.py concurrencyControlScan). No optimistic-concurrency header or version-token mechanism is documented anywhere in the pinned corpus for either transport. Journeys (Interactions) carry their own domain-specific versionNumber field (see deleteInteractionById.json), but that is a JOURNEY-VERSIONING feature (multiple named versions of one journey), not a generic optimistic-concurrency control usable for conflict detection on arbitrary writes.",
  "ConcurrencyControlConsequence": "No real MostRecent conflict resolution is possible from vendor-native concurrency tokens; conflict handling, if bidirectional is ever enabled, must be snapshot-3-way only (compare last-synced snapshot vs. current source vs. current destination), never an ETag/If-Match-based optimistic check.",
  "DeleteSemantics": {
    "default": "varies-by-object — NOT vendor-uniform, see per-family notes below",
    "contacts": {
      "shape": "async two-phase soft-then-hard",
      "evidence": "DeleteByContactIDs.json (POST /contacts/v1/contacts/actions/delete?type=ids) — EXPLICIT vendor statement: the call is asynchronous and QUEUED (''each process can take several hours to complete''); phase 1 is a SUPPRESSION (''A successful call suppresses any sends or other activities to the specified contacts for a specified number of days... Users may still see the contact record''); phase 2 after the configurable suppression window is an IRREVERSIBLE hard delete (''Marketing Cloud deletes the contact from all lists and sendable data extensions. You cannot restore any contact information associated with this delete process''). The suppression window is tenant-configurable via a separate ''Configure Settings for Deleting Contacts'' resource.",
      "softDeleteFlagField": "not a boolean flag — the tombstone state is implicit in the suppression window, not an inspectable field on the Contact record itself as far as this pinned corpus shows"
    },
    "dataExtensionRows": {
      "shape": "unproven-assume-hard",
      "evidence": "No explicit soft-delete/tombstone statement was located for SOAP DataExtensionObject Delete or the REST rowset delete path in this pass. Treat as immediate hard delete until independently confirmed; do NOT assume orphan-deletion is safe without a live-account check."
    },
    "genericSoapObjects": {
      "shape": "unproven-per-object",
      "evidence": "The CRUD matrix documents WHICH objects support a Delete verb (85 rows) but not the tombstone-vs-hard-delete SEMANTICS of that verb per object; this is a per-IO extraction task, not resolvable vendor-wide from the pinned corpus."
    },
    "consequence": "Orphan-deletion propagation must default OFF except where an object''s DeleteSemantics is independently proven hard/soft with evidence. Contacts specifically require respecting the tenant''s configured suppression window before treating a delete as final."
  },
  "DefaultSyncDirection": "Pull",
  "DefaultSyncDirectionNote": "Read-only Pull is the recommended default for EVERY object in this catalog per this build''s read-first, bidirectional-opt-in discipline. This is independent of any per-object WriteCapability finding above — write CAPABILITY existing at the vendor does not make write DEFAULT-safe against a client''s real Marketing Cloud tenant.",
  "DefaultConflictResolution": "Manual",
  "DefaultConflictResolutionNote": "IF bidirectional sync is later enabled for a specific object (opt-in, never default), the recommended conflict-resolution policy is Manual, NOT MostRecent — because ConcurrencyControl is ''none'' (no ETag/If-Match/version token found anywhere in the pinned corpus, see ConcurrencyControlEvidence), so there is no vendor-native mechanism to reliably determine which side is actually ''most recent'' at write time. DestWins/SourceWins are also not recommended as defaults because SFMC Contact deletes are irreversible after the suppression window (see DeleteSemantics.contacts) and SOAP writes on tracking-derived objects would corrupt system-of-record log data — a human must arbitrate. Preconditions before ANY bidirectional default could be safely reconsidered for a specific object: (1) that object''s WriteCapability is independently proven (not just CRUD-matrix-declared) against a live account, (2) that object''s DeleteSemantics is independently proven (hard vs. soft, and for Contacts specifically the exact suppression-window behavior) against a live account, (3) a sandbox validation pass exists (this vendor has NO free sandbox — see DeclaredCapabilityLimits no-free-sandbox — so this precondition is currently UNMET for every object in this catalog and bidirectional must stay opt-in-with-explicit-allowWrite for the foreseeable future).",
